// ============================================================================
// Hunt: GitHub "wfx-loot" campaign - credential theft through poisoned
//   GitHub Actions workflows (Aikido, disclosed November 12, 2026)
// MITRE ATT&CK: T1195.002 (Supply Chain Compromise: Compromise Software
//   Supply Chain), T1552.001 (Credentials In Files), T1552.004 (Private
//   Keys), T1550.001 (Application Access Token), T1078 (Valid Accounts),
//   T1071.001 (Web Protocols), T1041 (Exfiltration Over C2 Channel),
//   T1005 (Data from Local System)
// Data: Microsoft Defender DeviceFileEvents, DeviceProcessEvents
//   (Defender for Endpoint)
// Write-up: https://amitvijayan.com/journal/articles/daily-cyber-threat-brief-november-13-2026.html
// Logic: Aikido uncovered a campaign that began around 12:45 UTC on
//   November 10, 2026. Attackers used 4,837 compromised GitHub accounts
//   to probe 61,764 repositories for write access, opening pull requests
//   titled "docs: dependency audit notes" - 92% of which remain open.
//   Where a probe found write access, the attackers delivered a
//   1,500-line credential-stealing shell script by modifying an existing
//   GitHub Actions workflow, adding a new workflow on a short-lived
//   branch, or planting an npm install hook. The script harvested
//   GitHub Actions secrets and variables, the workflow's GITHUB_TOKEN,
//   OIDC tokens and Actions runtime tokens; AWS, Azure and Google Cloud
//   credentials, including credentials exposed through instance metadata
//   on self-hosted runners; Git, SSH, GPG, Docker, Kubernetes, npm,
//   PyPI, Vault and Terraform credentials; and AI service keys for
//   OpenAI, Anthropic, Gemini, Hugging Face, Cursor and GitHub Copilot.
//   It also swept environment variables, .env files, private keys,
//   keystores, shell histories, process memory, repository files, recent
//   commits and workflow artifacts, compressed the haul into tarballs
//   and exfiltrated it via HTTP POST to one of two attacker-controlled
//   endpoints, then reverted the changes and deleted workflow runs and
//   logs - all in under a minute. The script could also exchange GitHub
//   OIDC tokens for temporary cloud sessions, chain into additional IAM
//   roles in AWS to create long-lived credentials, impersonate identities
//   in GCP, and modify cloud identity and trust policies. Aikido
//   confirmed secrets stolen from at least 1,835 targets. About 50 of
//   the compromised accounts hold publishing rights on npm, PyPI, Go,
//   crates and Packagist, and automated publishing carried the payload
//   into three releases (Packagist digitalelvis/neuronai-studio 3.14.3
//   and two versions of the Go module github.com/onuragtas/docker-env).
//   Aikido notes overlaps with the 2025 GhostAction campaign. The
//   threat actor is unnamed, how the 4,837 credentials were originally
//   obtained is not known, and the two exfiltration endpoints were not
//   named in the reporting - so no actor IoCs are invented here. Every
//   query hunts the behaviour on self-hosted runners plus the named
//   GitHub-side markers in the comment below. Any hit on Variant 1 or 2
//   on a runner host means a workflow may have run the harvesting
//   script - treat every secret and cloud role available to that
//   workflow as compromised and rotate them, including OIDC trust
//   relationships, which the script was built to abuse.
// GitHub-side checks (audit log, not Defender): look for the "wfx-docs"
//   branch, pull requests titled "docs: dependency audit notes", and
//   gaps in workflow run numbers where runs and logs were deleted.
// ============================================================================

// -- Variant 1: a GitHub Actions runner process reading credential files --
// The harvesting script's core job is reading secrets off the runner:
// .env files, private keys, cloud credential stores, package-manager
// and infrastructure credential files. A build legitimately reads some
// of these, so this variant is scoped to the campaign window (7 days
// from the November 10 start) and to reads initiated by the runner
// worker/listener processes themselves. Triage any hit against the
// workflow that ran on that host at that time: a run whose workflow
// file was modified, a new workflow on a short-lived branch, or an
// npm install hook is the delivery pattern from the brief.
DeviceFileEvents
| where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
| where InitiatingProcessFileName in~ ("Runner.Worker.exe", "Runner.Worker", "Runner.Listener.exe", "Runner.Listener", "runsvc.sh")
| where FileName in~ (".env", "id_rsa", "id_ed25519", "id_ecdsa", "id_dsa", "credentials", ".npmrc", ".pypirc", ".dockercfg", "kubeconfig", "terraform.tfvars", ".netrc", ".git-credentials", ".bash_history", ".zsh_history")
    or FolderPath has_any (".aws", ".kube", ".ssh", ".docker", ".gnupg", ".terraform", ".config/gcloud")
| project TimeGenerated, DeviceName, ActionType, FileName, FolderPath, SHA256, InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine
| order by TimeGenerated desc

// -- Variant 2: a runner-launched process reaching cloud instance metadata --
// The script harvested credentials exposed through instance metadata on
// self-hosted runners. A normal build has no reason to query the cloud
// metadata service, so any process launched by the runner whose command
// line touches the metadata endpoint is a strong signal. The Google
// metadata hostname is included because the brief names GCP credentials
// among the harvested material.
DeviceProcessEvents
| where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
| where InitiatingProcessFileName in~ ("Runner.Worker.exe", "Runner.Worker", "Runner.Listener.exe", "Runner.Listener", "runsvc.sh")
| where ProcessCommandLine has_any ("169.254.169.254", "metadata.google.internal")
| project TimeGenerated, DeviceName, FileName, ProcessCommandLine, InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine, AccountName
| order by TimeGenerated desc

// -- Variant 3: a runner-launched process building or posting a tarball --
// The script compressed its haul into tarballs and exfiltrated them via
// HTTP POST. This variant lists tar, curl and wget processes launched by
// the runner whose command line references an archive or a POST body -
// the two halves of that exfiltration step. A tarball created on a
// runner shortly before a curl POST from the same host is the pattern
// to confirm; either half alone is worth a look in the campaign window.
DeviceProcessEvents
| where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
| where InitiatingProcessFileName in~ ("Runner.Worker.exe", "Runner.Worker", "Runner.Listener.exe", "Runner.Listener", "runsvc.sh")
| where FileName in~ ("tar", "curl", "curl.exe", "wget", "wget.exe")
| where ProcessCommandLine has_any (".tar", ".tgz", "tar.gz", "--data", "-d @", "-F @", "--upload-file")
| project TimeGenerated, DeviceName, FileName, ProcessCommandLine, InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine, AccountName
| order by TimeGenerated desc
